.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]requestStart\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]responseStart\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]firstInterimResponseStart\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]finalResponseHeadersStart\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]responseEnd\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]workerRouterEvaluationStart\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]workerCacheLookupStart\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]workerMatchedRouterSource\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]workerFinalRouterSource\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]transferSize\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]encodedBodySize\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]decodedBodySize\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]responseStatus\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]serverTiming\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
When the returned object is passed to \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], the
objects in the \f[CR]serverTiming\f[R] array are further serialized
using \f[CR]PerformanceServerTiming.toJSON()\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example obtains a \f[CR]PerformanceResourceTiming\f[R] object.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a plain object.
Its properties have the same values as the corresponding properties of
the original object.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  list.getEntries().forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] json = entry.toJSON();
    console.log(json); \f[I]// A plain object\f[R]
    console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
    console.log(json.initiatorType); \f[I]// Same value as entry.initiatorType\f[R]
  });
});

observer.observe({ type: \(dqresource\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
Within the callback from the previous example, the same object can be
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(entry));
.EE
.PP
This would log a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
